Info <p>This will automatically convert the body to json if possible when called.</p> <pre><code>response.body = {someKey: 'someValue'};

console.log(response.body);

// example output:
'{"someKey":"someValue"}'
</code></pre>

Info <p>This will NOT automatically convert the body to json if possible when called. This is great when working with an <code>afterAll</code> method that wants to mutate the body of the response before returning to the user.</p> <pre><code>response.rawBody = {someKey: 'someValue'};

console.log(response.rawBody);

// example output:
{
    someKey: 'someValue'
}
</code></pre>

Info <p>This will compress whatever is in the body property.</p> <pre><code>response.compress = true;

console.log(response.body);
// example output: this will gzip and compress the body.
</code></pre>

Info <p>Headers are case-sensitive, make sure your casing matches your expectations.</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post: {
        requiredHeaders: ['x-onbehalf-of']
    }
};
</code></pre>

Warning <p>This is not recommended for frequent use as it raises errors for every header which does not conform to the array provided. Many browsers, http tools, and libraries will automatically add headers to request, unbeknownst to the user. By using this setting, you will force every user of the endpoint to take extra care with the headers provided and may result in poor API consumer experience.</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post: {
        availableHeaders: ['x-onbehalf-of']
    }
};
</code></pre>

Warning <p>This is required if you are using dynamic routing (ex. <code>{id}.js</code>) with path parameters. The router will provide a path values in <code>request.pathParams</code></p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    put: {
        requiredPath: 'grower/{id}'
    }
};
</code></pre>

Info <p>This is referencing a <code>components.schemas</code> section of your openapi.yml file defined in the <code>schemaFile</code> value in your router config.</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post: {
        requiredBody: 'post-grower-request'
    }
};
</code></pre>

Info <p>This is referencing a <code>components.schemas</code> section of your openapi.yml file defined in the <code>schemaFile</code> value in your router config.</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post: {
        requiredResponse: 'post-grower-response'
    }
};
</code></pre>

Info <p>This will trigger the function you provided in the router config under the <code>withAuth</code> configuration</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post:{
        requiredAuth: true
    }
};
</code></pre>

Info <p>Instead of getting a <code>request</code> and <code>response</code> as arguments passed to your API function, you will get an instance of the class you provided here</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post: {
        dataClass: Grower
    }
};
</code></pre>

Info <p>This value will OVERRIDE any value set in the global timeout settings, set in the router config</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post: {
        timeout: 20000 // overrides other timeouts set in router config
    }
};
</code></pre>

Info <p>You can add as many custom requirements as you want, with any variable type you want, and they will be passed to your <code>beforeAll</code>, <code>before</code>, <code>afterAll</code>, <code>after</code> and <code>withAuth</code> middleware defined functions.</p> <pre><code>exports.requirements = {
    post:{
        myCustomBeforeAllPermission: {permission: 'allow-delete-grower'}
    }
};
</code></pre>

💡 Tip <p>When you introduce path parameters, name files using the <code>{param}.js</code> convention (for example <code>{farmId}.js</code>).</p> router.js <pre><code>const {Router} = require('acai').apigateway;

const router = new Router({
    basePath: 'api',               // optional: useful with custom domains
    handlerPath: 'api/handler'     // auto-expands to api/handler/**/*.js
});

router.autoLoad(); // optional cache shared across warm Lambdas

exports.route = async (event) =&gt; router.route(event);
</code></pre>

🧠 Pattern Ideas <p>Common globs include:</p> <ul> <li><code>api/**/*.controller.js</code></li> <li><code>src/**/handler.*.js</code></li> <li><code>services/**/endpoint.js</code></li> </ul> router.js <pre><code>const {Router} = require('acai').apigateway;
const router = new Router({
    basePath: 'api',
    handlerPattern: 'api/**/*.controller.js'
});

exports.route = async (event) =&gt; router.route(event);
</code></pre>

